<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Kizzuwatna</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 BCE – 1350 BCE (approx.) · capital Kummanni</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Kizzuwatna, a Hurrian-Luwian kingdom of the Cilician plain, through whose priests Hurrian gods and rituals entered Hittite religion.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Kizzuwatna arose in the plain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus</a> mountains after the Hittite <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/old-kingdom-of-egypt">Old Kingdom</a> weakened in the 16th century BCE. Its kings ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kummanni">Kummanni</a> and other centres such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adana">Adaniya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lawazantiya">Lawazantiya</a>, and balanced between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mitanni-empire">Mitanni Empire</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its kings, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isputahsu">Isputahsu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pilliya">Pilliya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunassura">Sunassura</a>, concluded treaties with Hittite rulers and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idrimi">Idrimi</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-mukish">Mukish</a>. The population mixed Luwian and Hurrian elements, and its temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teshub">Teshub</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebat">Hebat</a> and its priests specialised in purification rituals.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 1400 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunassura">Sunassura</a> accepted Hittite overlordship, and Kizzuwatna was soon absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a>. Its cults passed to the Hittite court; Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puduhepa">Puduhepa</a> was a priest's daughter from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lawazantiya">Lawazantiya</a>. The region later became Que.</p></div><aside class="st-side">